; Structured tasks with typed results across the process boundary
; (R-C2, docs/memory-model.md MM-PAR-13).
;
; WHAT IT IS. `taskMap f n width limit` runs `f i` for every `i` in
; `0 .. n`, each in a forked child, at most `width` at once, and answers
; one `(Result String Error)` per task in SUBMIT order. A task answers
; a `String`; what crosses back is its BYTES, copied out of the child
; into the parent's arena. That is the whole of the typed transfer: a
; program that wants a record, a `Vec`, a tree back encodes it into
; those bytes in the child and decodes them in the parent - JSON
; (`stdlib/Cereal.ax`) or a codec of its own. `tests/stdlib/542-task-codec.ax`
; carries a record with a `Vec` in it both ways and compares it with
; the sequential answer.
;
; WHY BYTES AND NOT A HEAP VALUE. A forked child's heap is a copy: a
; handle it answered would name the child's arena, which is gone once
; the child is (MM-PAR-3). No typed promotion of a heap value exists
; (MM-RGN-7 is planned), so serialization is not a workaround here, it
; is the transfer - explicit, bounded, and checked on arrival.
;
; WHY `__proc_spawn`, Par.ax's reason, unchanged: a task is a caller's
; closure, and its captures are only isolated by the forked lowering
; (MM-PAR-3), which this primitive names whatever `--threads` says. So
; a `--threads` program's tasks are processes too, and a task's
; captured `Vec` is the child's own copy.
;
; EVERY FAILURE IS A VALUE, and each has its own code:
;
;   Ok s                 the task answered `s`, at most `limit` bytes
;   Err 1..255           the task trapped or died: its wait status, an
;                        exit code or 128 plus the signal (Par.ax's
;                        convention); the runtime's message is on fd 2
;   Err taskTooLargeCode its answer was over `limit` bytes; nothing of
;                        it crossed, and the parent is unharmed
;   Err sysTimedOut      it ran past its deadline, and was SIGKILLed
;                        and reaped
;   Err taskCancelledCode the pool was cancelled: never started, or
;                        still running when the grace period ran out,
;                        and then SIGKILLed and reaped
;   Err 78 or 70         its spawn was refused - the kernel refused the
;                        fork or the handle table was full (78), or no
;                        page could be mapped for the handle (70) - and
;                        it never ran; the pool is then cancelled
;
; The codes above 255 cannot be mistaken for a wait status.
;
; BOUNDED, ALL OF IT. At most `width` children exist at once; the
; parent's per-slot state is a ring of `width` entries; each slot's
; answer is written into a MAP_SHARED slab of `width` slots of `limit`
; bytes, REUSED - O(width x limit), never O(n). Submission blocks
; while `width` tasks are outstanding: there is no queue. A task's slot
; is freed when its result is DELIVERED, in submit order, so a slow
; task holds back the tasks `width` places behind it (Par.ax's rule,
; for the same reason: the answer order is the submit order, and a
; bounded buffer cannot hold results out of order for ever). The
; answer of `taskMap` is O(n) because it is n results; `taskFold`
; streams them instead, each one live only while the fold's step runs,
; so memory stays flat however many tasks go through.
;
; DEADLINES, CANCELLATION, FAILURE.
;   * `deadline` (nanoseconds, 0 for none) runs from a task's spawn. A
;     task past it is sent SIGKILL and reaped, and answers
;     `sysTimedOut`; if it had already finished or died of its own
;     accord, that answer wins.
;   * A TOKEN is a `CancelToken` (`taskTokenNew`): a sealed handle
;     (docs/memory-model.md MM-VAL-10a, MM-PAR-8) naming one shared
;     page. `taskCancel` sets it from anywhere - another binding, a
;     task, the pool itself - and wakes every pool sleeping on it;
;     `taskCancelled` is the poll a cooperative task makes. It is
;     declared `shared`, so a `parallel` binding may capture it, and a
;     call on a freed one traps with status 85. Once a pool sees it set, it starts
;     nothing more (every unstarted task answers `taskCancelledCode`),
;     gives running tasks `grace` nanoseconds to finish, then SIGKILLs
;     and reaps what is left. A token the caller passes is shared state:
;     cancelling it cancels every pool using it.
;   * `failFast` sets the pool's token at the first task that answers
;     an error (not at a cancellation), which cancels the rest as above.
;   * A SPAWN THAT IS REFUSED cancels the pool too, without touching the
;     token: the refused task answers the refusal's status (78, or 70),
;     nothing more starts, running tasks get `grace` and are then
;     SIGKILLed and reaped, and the pool returns through its normal
;     path, so the slab is unmapped and a private token freed. The spawn
;     runs inside a recovery point of its own (`taskSpawn`), which is
;     how the runtime's refusal - a trap, MM-PAR-7 - comes back here as
;     a status instead of unwinding past the pool's cleanup.
;
; NO CHILD OUTLIVES THE CALL on any path the program has. A normal
; return joins every child it spawned. A trap in the parent during the
; pool - `taskFold`'s step, say - is MM-PAR-7's: every child is on the
; spawning thread's registry, and the unrecovered trap, or the recovery
; point armed outside the pool, SIGKILLs and reaps them
; (`scripts/check-task.sh` records the pids and requires them gone).
; What this cannot cover, stated: a parent killed by a signal it does
; not handle runs no sweep, so its tasks are reparented; a pool
; running inside a `parallel` binding that MM-PAR-7's sweep SIGKILLs
; cannot sweep its own children (MM-PAR-7's grandchildren limit); and
; under `--threads` the registry is per thread, so a pool's tasks
; outlive a trap in a SIBLING thread, which ends the process with only
; its own registry swept (and a raw `sysExitWith` sweeps nothing).
;
; HOW THE PARENT WAITS. It sleeps on the token's event counter
; (`sysWaitWordTimeout`), which a task bumps when its answer is in the
; slab and `taskCancel` bumps when it cancels - so a normal completion
; or a cancellation wakes it at once. A task that dies WITHOUT
; answering (a trap, a signal) bumps nothing, so the parent also looks
; at each running child every `taskPollNanos` (10 ms) with
; `sysChildExited` - `waitid` with WNOWAIT, which does not reap, so the
; join that follows still owns its child. Where no such look exists
; (`sysChildExited` answers `Err`), a death without an answer is found
; at the task's deadline, or - with no deadline - by blocking on the
; oldest running task's join when nothing else can move, and a
; cancellation then waits for that join.
;
; A TASK IS KILLED BY ITS PID, which `__spawn_pid` reads from the page
; its `Spawn` handle names, through the runtime's handle table
; (MM-PAR-8): the page layout stays the runtime's, and a handle already
; joined traps with status 85 instead of reading an unmapped page.
; `scripts/check-task.sh` checks the pid through the deadline's kill:
; with any other number, the task outlives its deadline and the check
; goes red (the `layout` ablation).
;
; DETERMINISM. The RESULTS are in submit order and depend only on what
; each task answered - never on scheduling. SIDE EFFECTS are not: tasks
; run concurrently, so their writes to fd 1, to files or to a shared
; mapping interleave however the scheduler ran them, and a deadline or
; cancellation decides by the clock. On Darwin that clock is the
; realtime one (`sysTimeoutMicros`), so a step of it moves every running
; deadline by the step.
(import Mem)
(import Str)
(import Vec)
(import Sys)
(import Err)

; ---- the options ---------------------------------------------------------
; `width` children at most (clamped to 1..n); `limit` bytes per answer
; (0 or more); `deadline` nanoseconds per task from its spawn, 0 for
; none; `grace` nanoseconds a running task gets after a cancellation;
; `failFast` cancels the pool at the first error; `token` is `Some` a
; token from `taskTokenNew`, or `None` for one private to the call.
(pub struct TaskOpts
  (width : Int)
  (limit : Int)
  (deadline : Int)
  (grace : Int)
  (failFast : Bool)
  (token : (Option CancelToken)))

; No deadline, a 100 ms grace, no fail-fast, a private token.
(pub :: taskOpts (-> Int Int TaskOpts))
(pub fn (taskOpts width limit)
  (TaskOpts
    width
    limit
    0
    100000000
    false
    None))

(pub :: taskWithDeadline (-> TaskOpts Int TaskOpts))
(pub fn (taskWithDeadline o nanos)
  (TaskOpts
    o.width
    o.limit
    nanos
    o.grace
    o.failFast
    o.token))

(pub :: taskWithGrace (-> TaskOpts Int TaskOpts))
(pub fn (taskWithGrace o nanos)
  (TaskOpts
    o.width
    o.limit
    o.deadline
    nanos
    o.failFast
    o.token))

(pub :: taskWithFailFast (-> TaskOpts Bool TaskOpts))
(pub fn (taskWithFailFast o on)
  (TaskOpts
    o.width
    o.limit
    o.deadline
    o.grace
    on
    o.token))

(pub :: taskWithToken (-> TaskOpts CancelToken TaskOpts))
(pub fn (taskWithToken o tok)
  (TaskOpts
    o.width
    o.limit
    o.deadline
    o.grace
    o.failFast
    (Some tok)))

; ---- the codes -------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: taskCancelledCode Int)
(pub fn (taskCancelledCode)
  1002)

(pub :: taskTooLargeCode Int)
(pub fn (taskTooLargeCode)
  1003)

; How often a sleeping pool looks at its running children: 10 ms.
(pub :: taskPollNanos Int)
(pub fn (taskPollNanos)
  10000000)

; The first look after a task has answered and not yet exited: 20 us.
; Its process is normally microseconds from its `exit`, so a fixed
; millisecond here held every single-task pool for one (a p50 round
; trip of 1.4 ms against 0.24 ms before, `scripts/bench-concurrency.sh`).
; Each look that finds nothing doubles it, up to `taskPollNanos`, so a
; task that answered and cannot exit costs no busy loop while its
; deadline runs.
(:: taskExitNapFirst Int)
(fn (taskExitNapFirst)
  20000)

(:: taskNapNext (-> Int Int))
(fn (taskNapNext nap)
  (if (> (* nap 2) taskPollNanos)
    taskPollNanos
    (* nap 2)))

; The poll period: `nap` while some task has answered and not yet exited
; (`answered` 1), else the usual one.
(:: taskPollPeriod (-> Int Int Int))
(fn (taskPollPeriod answered nap)
  (if (== answered 1)
    nap
    taskPollNanos))

; ---- the raw words ---------------------------------------------------------
; The module's only raw accesses, each `effect(unsafe)`; every public
; function goes through them and claims no more than `effect(io)`.
(:: taskLoadAt (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskLoadAt a)
  (__atomic_load a))

(:: taskStoreAt (-> Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskStoreAt a v)
  {
    (__atomic_store a v)
    0
  })

(:: taskAddAt (-> Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskAddAt a v)
  (__atomic_add a v))

; The pid a task runs as, read through its live `Spawn` handle.
(:: taskHandlePid (-> Spawn Int))
(fn (taskHandlePid h)
  (__spawn_pid h))

; A block with a share taken, and the share given back.
(:: taskScratch (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskScratch n)
  (let ((b (memAlloc n)))
    {
      (__retain b)
      b
    }))

(:: taskScratchDone (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
;@axiom:precondition(`b` is a block `taskScratch` answered, handed back once and not used after)
(fn (taskScratchDone b)
  {
    (__release b)
    0
  })

; ---- the token -------------------------------------------------------------
; A cancellation token: one word, a slot in the runtime's handle table
; (MM-PAR-8) naming a shared page - word 0 the cancelled flag, word 1
; the event counter a pool sleeps on.
(pub struct CancelToken word shared
  (slot : Int))

; The handle table's kind for a token (`Chan.ax`'s is 2, `Sync.ax`'s 3).
(:: taskKind Int)
(fn (taskKind)
  4)

; The page a live token names, or the 85 trap for one that was freed.
(:: taskTokenAt (-> CancelToken Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskTokenAt t)
  (__handle_get taskKind t.slot))

; A handle for a fresh page, or 0 when the table has no slot left.
(:: taskTokenName (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskTokenName page)
  (__handle_new taskKind page))

; Retire a live token's handle and answer its page, or the 85 trap for a
; token already freed.
(:: taskTokenRetire (-> CancelToken Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskTokenRetire t)
  (__handle_free taskKind t.slot))

; A fresh token, not set. `Err` the mapping's error, or EMFILE (24) when
; the handle table has no slot left.
(pub :: taskTokenNew (Result CancelToken Error))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (taskTokenNew)
  (match (sysMapShared 4096)
    ((Ok page) (taskTokenNamed page))
    ((Err e) (Err e))))

; Name a fresh page in the handle table, or unmap it and say why not.
(:: taskTokenNamed (-> Int (Result CancelToken Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskTokenNamed page)
  (let ((h (taskTokenName page)))
    (if (== h 0)
      (match (sysUnmapShared page 4096)
        ((Ok z) (Err (mkError 24 "taskTokenNew: every handle is in use")))
        ((Err e) (Err (mkError 24 "taskTokenNew: every handle is in use"))))
      (Ok (CancelToken h)))))

; Set the token and wake every pool sleeping on it. Idempotent.
(pub :: taskCancel (-> CancelToken Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (taskCancel t)
  (taskCancelAt (taskTokenAt t)))

; Whether the token is set: the poll a cooperative task makes.
(pub :: taskCancelled (-> CancelToken Bool))
(pub fn (taskCancelled t)
  (taskCancelledAt (taskTokenAt t)))

; Unmap a token. Only once no pool and no task can still reach it; the
; handle is retired first, so every call after this one - a second
; `taskTokenFree` included - traps with status 85.
(pub :: taskTokenFree (-> CancelToken (Result Int Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
;@axiom:precondition(no pool or task can still use this token: every concurrent user has joined before the call)
(pub fn (taskTokenFree t)
  (sysUnmapShared (taskTokenRetire t) 4096))

; The two halves above, on the page itself: what the pool and its tasks
; use once the token's page is in hand.
(:: taskCancelAt (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (taskCancelAt tok)
  {
    (taskStoreAt tok 1)
    (taskBump tok)
  })

(:: taskCancelledAt (-> Int Bool))
(fn (taskCancelledAt tok)
  (== (taskLoadAt tok) 1))

(:: taskBump (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (taskBump tok)
  {
    (taskAddAt (+ tok 8) 1)
    (sysWakeWord (+ tok 8))
  })

(:: taskEvent (-> Int Int))
(fn (taskEvent tok)
  (taskLoadAt (+ tok 8)))

; ---- the child's half ----------------------------------------------------
; A slot of the slab: word 0 the state (1 running, 2 answered, 3 over
; the limit), word 1 the answer's length, word 2 the child's pid as the
; child saw it, then the answer's bytes from byte 32.
;
; `arg` is `t * w + s`: task `t` in slot `s`, one word because a spawn
; hands its thunk one. Runs in the CHILD; the state is stored after the
; bytes, and the parent reads the bytes only after the join, which
; orders them (MM-PAR-9, edge 3) - the state is the wake-up, not the
; publication.
(:: taskChild (-> (-> Int String) Int Int Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskChild f slab slotBytes limit tok w arg)
  (let ((slot (+ slab (* (% arg w) slotBytes))))
    {
      (memSetWord slot 2 sysGetPid)
      (let ((r (f (/ arg w))))
        (let ((len (strLen r)))
          {
            (memSetWord slot 1 len)
            (if (> len limit)
              (taskStoreAt slot 3)
              {
                (memCopy (+ slot 32) (strData r) len)
                (taskStoreAt slot 2)
              })
            (taskBump tok)
            0
          }))
    }))

; ---- the parent's half ---------------------------------------------------
; Per slot, three words of `st`: the deadline (microseconds on
; `sysTimeoutMicros`'s clock, 0 for none), the wait status, and why it
; was joined - 0 not yet, 1 it ended, 2 killed at its deadline, 3 killed
; at the end of a cancellation's grace. The slot's `Spawn` handle is
; word `s` of `hs`.
(:: taskSt (-> (Vec Int) Int Int Int))
(fn (taskSt st s k)
  (vecGet st (+ (* 3 s) k)))

(:: taskSetSt (-> (Vec Int) Int Int Int Int))
(fn (taskSetSt st s k v)
  {
    (vecSet st (+ (* 3 s) k) v)
    0
  })

; Join slot `s`'s child - it has ended, or was just killed - and record
; why. Answers 1 when the outcome is an error `failFast` counts: not a
; cancellation's kill.
(:: taskJoin (-> (Vec Int) (Vec Spawn) Int Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (taskJoin st hs s cell slab slotBytes why)
  (let ((w (__proc_join_nr (vecGet hs s) cell)))
    (let (
      (status (memGetWord cell 0))
      (state (taskLoadAt (+ slab (* s slotBytes))))
    )
      {
        (taskSetSt st s 1 status)
        (taskSetSt st s 2 why)
        ; The same `finished` test `taskResultAt` makes: a task that
        ; answered and was then SIGKILLed from outside (137, joined as
        ; ended) is delivered as `Err 137`, so it counts for `failFast`.
        (if (== why 3)
          0
          (if (&& (== state 2) (|| (== status 0) (&& (== status 137) (>= why 2))))
            0
            1))
      })))

; Kill slot `s`'s child and join it.
(:: taskKillJoin (-> (Vec Int) (Vec Spawn) Int Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (taskKillJoin st hs s cell slab slotBytes why)
  {
    (match (sysKill (taskHandlePid (vecGet hs s)) 9)
      ((Ok z) 0)
      ((Err e) 0))
    (taskJoin
      st
      hs
      s
      cell
      slab
      slotBytes
      why)
  })

; The answer of a joined slot, built in the caller's current region.
; `slot` is the slab slot, `status` the joined child's wait status and
; `why` why it was joined (`taskJoin`), or 4 when its spawn was refused
; and `status` is the refusal's.
(:: taskResultAt (-> Int Int Int (Result String Error)))
(fn (taskResultAt slot status why)
  (let (
    (state (taskLoadAt slot))
    (finished (|| (== status 0) (&& (== status 137) (>= why 2))))
  )
    (if (== why 4)
      (Err (mkError status "task not started: its spawn was refused"))
      (if (&& finished (== state 2))
        (Ok (taskCopyOut slot))
        (if (&& finished (== state 3))
          (Err (mkError taskTooLargeCode "task answer over the byte limit"))
          (if (== status 0)
            (Err (mkError 78 "task ended without an answer"))
            (if (&& (== status 137) (== why 2))
              (Err (mkError sysTimedOut "task past its deadline"))
              (if (&& (== status 137) (== why 3))
                (Err (mkError taskCancelledCode "task cancelled"))
                (Err (mkError status "task trapped"))))))))))

(:: taskCopyOut (-> Int String))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskCopyOut slot)
  (let ((len (memGetWord slot 1)))
    (let ((s (strAlloc len)))
      {
        (memCopy (strData s) (+ slot 32) len)
        s
      })))

; `scoped` is zero for retained answers, or a retained three-word
; mark cell for taskFold. The pool and its captures predate each mark.
; The fold's caller promises that the sink retains no scoped allocation.
; Refreshing the cell allocates nothing, so deliveries leave no marks.
(:: taskDeliver (-> (-> (Result String Error) Int) Int (Vec Int) Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskDeliver sink scoped st s slab slotBytes)
  (let (
    (slot (+ slab (* s slotBytes)))
    (status (taskSt st s 1))
    (why (taskSt st s 2))
  )
    (if (!= scoped 0)
      {
        (__axiom_arena_mark_into scoped)
        (sink (taskResultAt slot status why))
        (__axiom_arena_reset scoped)
      }
      (sink (taskResultAt slot status why)))))

(:: taskDeliverCancelled (-> (-> (Result String Error) Int) Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskDeliverCancelled sink scoped)
  (if (!= scoped 0)
    {
      (__axiom_arena_mark_into scoped)
      (sink (Err (mkError taskCancelledCode "task not started: the pool was cancelled")))
      (__axiom_arena_reset scoped)
    }
    (sink (Err (mkError taskCancelledCode "task not started: the pool was cancelled")))))

; Earliest instant, in microseconds, the parent must wake by: the
; nearest deadline of a running task, the end of a cancellation's
; grace, or `now` plus the poll period - or -1 when none applies. A task
; that has answered and not yet exited is normally a few microseconds
; from its exit, so while one exists the period is `nap`, the backoff
; `taskLoop` keeps.
(:: taskWakeBy (-> (Vec Int) Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int))
(fn (taskWakeBy st head next w now canPoll cancelling graceEnd slab slotBytes nap)
  (let (
    (mut wake (if (== cancelling 1)
      graceEnd
      (- 0 1)))
    (mut running 0)
    (mut answered 0)
  )
    {
      (for t in head..next
        (let ((s (% t w)))
          (if (== (taskSt st s 2) 0)
            {
              (set running 1)
              (if (>= (taskLoadAt (+ slab (* s slotBytes))) 2)
                (set answered 1)
                0)
              (let ((d (taskSt st s 0)))
                (if (&& (> d 0) (|| (< wake 0) (< d wake)))
                  (set wake d)
                  0))
            }
            0)))
      (if (== running 0)
        (- 0 1)
        (if (== canPoll 1)
          (let ((p (+ now (/ (taskPollPeriod answered nap) 1000))))
            (if (|| (< wake 0) (< p wake))
              p
              wake))
          wake))
    }))

; Has slot `s`'s child ended? 1 yes - the kernel says it has exited, or
; it answered and there is no look to ask - 2 it answered but has not
; exited yet, 0 not seen, -1 the look itself failed (`sysChildExited`
; answered `Err`; the caller stops asking). An answer alone is not an
; end: a task whose `f` returned can still be held up - by a thread its
; own `parallel` never joins, say - and a join then would block the
; whole pool in the kernel with no deadline or grace enforced. So an
; answered task is joined when the kernel reports its exit, and until
; then stays under its deadline and a cancellation's grace like any
; other running task.
(:: taskEnded (-> (Vec Spawn) Int Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (taskEnded hs s slab slotBytes pbuf canPoll)
  (let ((answered (>= (taskLoadAt (+ slab (* s slotBytes))) 2)))
    (if (== canPoll 1)
      (match (sysChildExited (taskHandlePid (vecGet hs s)) pbuf)
        ((Ok b)
          (if b
            1
            (if answered
              2
              0))
        )
        ((Err e)
          (if answered
            1
            (- 0 1))
        ))
      (if answered
        1
        0))))

; Look at running slot `s` once, at `now`: join it if it ended, kill
; and join it if its deadline or a cancellation's grace has run out.
; Answers -1 nothing happened, -2 the look at the child failed, else
; `taskJoin`'s answer (1 an error `failFast` counts, 0 not).
(:: taskCheck (-> (Vec Int) (Vec Spawn) Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (taskCheck st hs s now cancelling graceEnd canPoll cell slab slotBytes pbuf)
  (let ((ended (taskEnded
    hs
    s
    slab
    slotBytes
    pbuf
    canPoll)))
    (if (== ended 1)
      (taskJoin
        st
        hs
        s
        cell
        slab
        slotBytes
        1)
      (if (&& (> (taskSt st s 0) 0) (>= now (taskSt st s 0)))
        (taskKillJoin
          st
          hs
          s
          cell
          slab
          slotBytes
          2)
        (if (&& (== cancelling 1) (>= now graceEnd))
          (taskKillJoin
            st
            hs
            s
            cell
            slab
            slotBytes
            3)
          (if (== ended (- 0 1))
            (- 0 2)
            (- 0 1)))))))

; Spawn `wrapper` on `arg` inside a recovery point of its own, so a
; refused spawn comes back here as a status rather than trapping past
; the pool (MM-PAR-7's 78). Answers the handle's word, or the status:
; 78 when the kernel refused the fork or the handle table was full, 70
; when the handle's page could not be mapped. The runtime has already
; given back the page and the thunk's share by then, and a child
; spawned in the extent is swept by the abort, but none can be: the
; spawn is all it does. Its one allocation, the mark, is taken back by
; the `region` around it.
(:: taskSpawn (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
(fn (taskSpawn wrapper arg)
  (region r
    (__axiom_recover
      __axiom_arena_mark
      (lambda (x) (taskWordOf (__proc_spawn wrapper arg))))))

; Whether `taskSpawn` answered a handle: a handle's word is its slot's
; generation, at least 1, times 2^16 plus the slot, and a status is
; below 256.
(:: taskSpawned (-> Int Bool))
(fn (taskSpawned hw)
  (>= hw 65536))

; A spawn handle as the word the recovery point can answer, and back.
; The way back makes a `Spawn` out of a word, so the word must be one
; `taskSpawn` answered and `taskSpawned` accepted: a `cast` to `Spawn` is
; not refused as an unsafe operation today (R-C6's limit), so that
; obligation is this module's, and this is its one such cast.
(:: taskWordOf (-> Spawn Int))
(fn (taskWordOf h)
  (cast Int h))

(:: taskHandleOf (-> Int Spawn))
(fn (taskHandleOf hw)
  (cast Spawn hw))

; Start task `t` in its slot `t % w`. Its deadline runs from the clock
; read AFTER the spawn answered, so a task started late in a batch -
; after other forks - keeps the whole of its deadline. Slot `s`'s first
; task pushes its handle onto `hs`, which grows to `w` in slot order;
; every later one replaces the handle joined before it. Answers 0, or
; the status of a refused spawn, which the slot then holds as joined
; with `why` 4: nothing ran, and `hs` is not touched.
(:: taskStart (-> (Vec Int) (Vec Spawn) (-> Int Int) Int Int Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
(fn (taskStart st hs wrapper t w slab slotBytes clk deadlineUs)
  (let (
    (s (% t w))
    (slot (+ slab (* (% t w) slotBytes)))
  )
    {
      (taskStoreAt slot 1)
      (memSetWord slot 1 0)
      (memSetWord slot 2 0)
      (let ((hw (taskSpawn wrapper (+ (* t w) s))))
        (if (taskSpawned hw)
          {
            (let ((h (taskHandleOf hw)))
              (if (< t w)
                (vecPush hs h)
                (vecSet hs s h)))
            (taskSetSt
              st
              s
              0
              (if (> deadlineUs 0)
                (+ (sysTimeoutMicros clk) deadlineUs)
                0))
            (taskSetSt st s 1 0)
            (taskSetSt st s 2 0)
            0
          }
          {
            (taskSetSt st s 0 0)
            (taskSetSt st s 1 hw)
            (taskSetSt st s 2 4)
            hw
          }))
    }))

; Nanoseconds from `now` to `wake` (microseconds), at least one, and at
; most 2^62: the product must not wrap into a negative sleep.
(:: taskSleepFor (-> Int Int Int))
(fn (taskSleepFor now wake)
  (if (> wake now)
    (if (> (- wake now) 4611686018427387)
      4611686018427387000
      (* (- wake now) 1000))
    1000))

; Microseconds for a nanosecond option, rounded up; 0 stays 0. A value
; within 999 of the largest `Int` must not wrap as it rounds - a grace
; of "for ever" would read as a negative one and kill at once.
(:: taskMicros (-> Int Int))
(fn (taskMicros nanos)
  (if (> nanos 0)
    (if (> nanos 9223372036854774808)
      9223372036854776
      (/ (+ nanos 999) 1000))
    0))

; The pool itself. Answers how many results it delivered (n).
(:: taskLoop (-> (-> Int String) Int TaskOpts (-> (Result String Error) Int) Int Int Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (taskLoop f n opts sink scoped w limit slotBytes tok slab)
  (let (
    (st (vecWithCapacity (* 3 w)))
    (hs (vecWithCapacity w))
    (cell (taskScratch 8))
    (clk (taskScratch 16))
    (pbuf (taskScratch sysChildPollBytes))
    (wrapper (lambda (arg) (taskChild
      f
      slab
      slotBytes
      limit
      tok
      w
      arg)))
    (deadlineUs (taskMicros opts.deadline))
    (graceUs (taskMicros opts.grace))
    (mut next 0)
    (mut head 0)
    (mut cancelling 0)
    (mut graceEnd 0)
    (mut canPoll 1)
    (mut progress 0)
    (mut nap taskExitNapFirst)
  )
    {
      (for i in 0..(* 3 w)
        (vecPush st 0))
      (while (< head n)
        (let (
          (seen (taskEvent tok))
          (now (sysTimeoutMicros clk))
        )
          {
            (set progress 0)
            ; A cancellation seen for the first time.
            (if (&& (== cancelling 0) (taskCancelledAt tok))
              {
                (set cancelling 1)
                (set graceEnd (+ now graceUs))
                (set progress 1)
              }
              0)
            ; Start what the window allows. A refused spawn cancels the
            ; pool as a cancellation does, and leaves the token alone.
            (while (&& (== cancelling 0) (&& (< next n) (< (- next head) w)))
              {
                (if (!= (taskStart
                  st
                  hs
                  wrapper
                  next
                  w
                  slab
                  slotBytes
                  clk
                  deadlineUs) 0)
                  {
                    (set cancelling 1)
                    (set graceEnd (+ now graceUs))
                  }
                  0)
                (set next (+ next 1))
                (set progress 1)
              })
            ; Join what has ended; kill and join what ran out of time.
            (for t in head..next
              (let ((s (% t w)))
                (if (== (taskSt st s 2) 0)
                  (let ((r (taskCheck
                    st
                    hs
                    s
                    now
                    cancelling
                    graceEnd
                    canPoll
                    cell
                    slab
                    slotBytes
                    pbuf)))
                    (if (== r (- 0 2))
                      (set canPoll 0)
                      (if (>= r 0)
                        {
                          (set progress 1)
                          (if (&& (== r 1) opts.failFast)
                            (taskCancelAt tok)
                            0)
                        }
                        0)))
                  0)))
            ; Deliver, in submit order, what has been joined.
            (while (&& (< head next) (!= (taskSt st (% head w) 2) 0))
              {
                (taskDeliver
                  sink
                  scoped
                  st
                  (% head w)
                  slab
                  slotBytes)
                (set head (+ head 1))
                (set progress 1)
              })
            ; Once cancelled, what was never started answers so.
            (while (&& (== cancelling 1) (&& (== head next) (< next n)))
              {
                (taskDeliverCancelled sink scoped)
                (set next (+ next 1))
                (set head (+ head 1))
                (set progress 1)
              })
            ; Nothing moved: sleep until something can. The exit backoff
            ; grows with each empty look and starts again on progress.
            (if (== progress 0)
              (let ((wake (taskWakeBy
                st
                head
                next
                w
                now
                canPoll
                cancelling
                graceEnd
                slab
                slotBytes
                nap)))
                {
                  (set nap (taskNapNext nap))
                  (if (>= wake 0)
                    (sysWaitWordTimeout (+ tok 8) seen (taskSleepFor now wake))
                    ; No clock can end the wait and no look at a child
                    ; exists: block on the oldest running task's join.
                    (if (&& (< head next) (== (taskSt st (% head w) 2) 0))
                      (let ((r (taskJoin
                        st
                        hs
                        (% head w)
                        cell
                        slab
                        slotBytes
                        1)))
                        (if (&& (== r 1) opts.failFast)
                          (taskCancelAt tok)
                          0))
                      0))
                })
              (set nap taskExitNapFirst))
          }))
      (taskScratchDone cell)
      (taskScratchDone clk)
      (taskScratchDone pbuf)
      (vecFree st)
      (vecFree hs)
      head
    }))

; Every task answers `e`: the pool could not be set up.
(:: taskAllFailed (-> Int Error (-> (Result String Error) Int) Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskAllFailed n e sink scoped)
  {
    (for i in 0..n
      (if (!= scoped 0)
        {
          (__axiom_arena_mark_into scoped)
          (sink (Err e))
          (__axiom_arena_reset scoped)
        }
        (sink (Err e))))
    n
  })

; The pool with its slab mapped around it.
(:: taskWithSlab (-> (-> Int String) Int TaskOpts (-> (Result String Error) Int) Int Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (taskWithSlab f n opts sink scoped w limit slotBytes tok)
  (match (sysMapShared (* w slotBytes))
    ((Err e) (taskAllFailed n e sink scoped))
    ((Ok slab)
      (let ((done (taskLoop
        f
        n
        opts
        sink
        scoped
        w
        limit
        slotBytes
        tok
        slab)))
        (match (sysUnmapShared slab (* w slotBytes))
          ((Ok z) done)
          ((Err e) done)))
    )))

; The token the pool sleeps on: the caller's, or a fresh one.
(:: taskTokenFor (-> (Option CancelToken) (Result CancelToken Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (taskTokenFor tok)
  (match tok
    ((Some t) (Ok t))
    ((None) taskTokenNew)))

; Whether the pool made its token itself, and so frees it.
(:: taskOwnsToken (-> (Option CancelToken) Bool))
(fn (taskOwnsToken tok)
  (match tok
    ((Some t) false)
    ((None) true)))

; The width actually used: at least 1, at most n.
(:: taskWidth (-> Int Int Int))
(fn (taskWidth width n)
  (if (< width 1)
    1
    (if (> width n)
      n
      width)))

; The pool with its mappings made and unmade around it.
(:: taskRun (-> (-> Int String) Int TaskOpts (-> (Result String Error) Int) Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (taskRun f n opts sink scoped)
  (if (<= n 0)
    0
    (let (
      (w (taskWidth opts.width n))
      (limit (if (< opts.limit 0)
        0
        opts.limit))
    )
      (if (> limit 1099511627776)
        (taskAllFailed n (mkError 22 "task limit over 2^40 bytes") sink scoped)
        (match (taskTokenFor opts.token)
          ((Err e) (taskAllFailed n e sink scoped))
          ((Ok tok)
            (let ((done (taskWithSlab
              f
              n
              opts
              sink
              scoped
              w
              limit
              (+ 32 (* 8 (/ (+ limit 7) 8)))
              (taskTokenAt tok))))
              (if (taskOwnsToken opts.token)
                (match (taskTokenFree tok)
                  ((Ok z) done)
                  ((Err e) done))
                done))
          ))))))

; ---- the entries ---------------------------------------------------------
; `f i` for every `i` in `0 .. n`, at most `width` at once, each answer
; at most `limit` bytes: one `Result` per task in submit order. No
; deadline, no fail-fast, a private token.
(pub :: taskMap (-> (-> Int String) Int Int Int (Vec (Result String Error))))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (taskMap f n width limit)
  (taskMapWith f n (taskOpts width limit)))

; `taskMap` with every option (`TaskOpts`).
(pub :: taskMapWith (-> (-> Int String) Int TaskOpts (Vec (Result String Error))))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (taskMapWith f n opts)
  (let ((out vecNew))
    {
      (taskRun
        f
        n
        opts
        (lambda (r)
          {
            (vecPush out r)
            0
          }
        )
        0)
      out
    }))

; Decode each successful answer in the parent, in submit order. Task
; failures pass through unchanged and never call `decode`. The encoded
; answers keep `taskMap`'s byte limit; decoded storage is the decoder's
; responsibility and the result vector holds n values.
(pub :: taskMapDecoded (-> (-> Int String) (-> String (Result a Error)) Int Int Int (Vec (Result a Error))))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (taskMapDecoded f decode n width limit)
  (taskMapDecodedWith f decode n (taskOpts width limit)))

; All task options, with parent-side decoding. Decoder errors belong to
; their result slots; `failFast` concerns worker failures, not decoding.
; Delivery calls `decode` before starting more work, so a slow decoder
; can delay submission and deadline/cancellation handling.
(pub :: taskMapDecodedWith (-> (-> Int String) (-> String (Result a Error)) Int TaskOpts (Vec (Result a Error))))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (taskMapDecodedWith f decode n opts)
  (let ((out vecNew))
    {
      (taskRun
        f
        n
        opts
        (lambda (r)
          {
            (vecPush out (taskDecodeResult decode r))
            0
          }
        )
        0)
      out
    }))

(:: taskDecodeResult (-> (-> String (Result a Error)) (Result String Error) (Result a Error)))
(fn (taskDecodeResult decode r)
  (match r
    ((Ok s) (decode s))
    ((Err e) (Err e))))

; Fold the answers in submit order without keeping them: `step acc i r`
; for each task, starting from `init`, answering the last `acc`.
; Each answer and everything the step allocates are reclaimed on return.
; A retained mark cell is refreshed before delivery, then reset after it.
; The pool's state and the accumulator were allocated before that mark.
;
; The caller must keep only scalar accumulator bits across a step.
; Saving an answer or growing a captured Vec would retain reclaimed
; memory. This is taskFold's explicit Unsafe precondition; checked
; regions refuse such opaque callbacks because captures may be stores.
; taskMapWith retains answers and has no scoped callback contract.
(pub :: taskFold (-> (-> Int String) Int TaskOpts Int (-> Int Int (Result String Error) Int) Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
;@axiom:precondition(`step` keeps only what its `Int` accumulator carries: an answer or anything it allocates is reclaimed when it returns)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (taskFold f n opts init step)
  (let (
    (acc (taskScratch 16))
    (mark (taskScratch 24))
  )
    {
      (memSetWord acc 0 init)
      (memSetWord acc 1 0)
      (taskRun
        f
        n
        opts
        (lambda (r) (taskFoldOne acc step r))
        mark)
      (let ((v (memGetWord acc 0)))
        {
          (taskScratchDone acc)
          (taskScratchDone mark)
          v
        })
    }))

; One step: word 0 of `acc` is the scalar accumulator, word 1 the
; submit-order index. Neither word may retain a scoped allocation.
(:: taskFoldOne (-> Int (-> Int Int (Result String Error) Int) (Result String Error) Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (taskFoldOne acc step r)
  (let ((i (memGetWord acc 1)))
    (let ((g (step (memGetWord acc 0) i)))
      {
        (memSetWord acc 1 (+ i 1))
        (memSetWord acc 0 (g r))
      })))
